<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Meskalamdug</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2600–2500 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Meskalamdug was a ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> around 2600–2500 BCE, known from finds in the Royal Cemetery excavated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonard-woolley">Leonard Woolley</a>. A grave holding his name yielded a celebrated helmet beaten from a single sheet of gold in the form of a wig, now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-museum-of-iraq">Iraq Museum</a>. A seal names him as king, and he may have been the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesannepadda">Mesannepadda</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty</a> of Ur.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meskalamdug.jpg" alt="Meskalamdug" width="320" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Akieboy · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Golden_helmet_of_Meskalamdug_in_the_British_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
